-- the quire goes in --
chapters stored: 3
Colophon, first edition:  ImportOk
Impression:               ImportOk
Colophon, second edition: ImportOk

-- the colophon can be checked, which is new --
the note names the hash the store filed it under: yes
and the store agrees:                             yes
that same note about the SECOND edition:          refused

-- both editions are still here, and both verify --
  first:  Chapter: Colophon
  second: Chapter: Colophon
A content-addressed store cannot lose an edition. It does not overwrite; it
accumulates, and every edition it was ever given is still exactly itself.

-- ask the KERNEL for a chapter by name, and it answers the wrong question --
app-scan-last takes a KIND, not a name, so it hands back the last fact of kind
30 -- the last source file written, whichever chapter that happened to be.
  asked for Impression, the kernel offers: Colophon (the last one written)
It does not fail. It answers a question nobody asked, which is worse. And
DiskFacts' own index cannot help: disk-load keys each fact by the hash of the
stored LINE, not of the content that line describes, so a source body cannot
be found by its own content hash at all.
  disk-read-fact, by the body's true hash: not found
That is an index of records. A repository needs an index of works.

-- so here is one --
the tree: 2 works
  apps/colophon/Colophon.codex  |  apps/colophon/Impression.codex

ask it BY NAME and it answers the question you asked:
  apps/colophon/Impression.codex -> Impression, 54 bytes
  apps/colophon/Colophon.codex   -> Colophon, 129 bytes

and the current Colophon is the SECOND edition, because the last write for a
path is the current value of that path. That is what a log has always meant,
and it needs no supersession record to say it:
  current == the second edition: yes

SUPERSEDING IS NOT DELETING. The first edition is still here, still verifies,
and always will. It is simply no longer what the path points at:
  the first edition, fetched by its hash: Colophon, 118 bytes
  and it still hashes to itself:          yes

-- what is still missing --
There is no way to say a path is GONE. A tombstone, and the removal it would
let you express, is 6.5. Until then this store can add and it can revise, and
it cannot forget -- which, for a repository, is the right way round to be
incomplete.
